>Yes. Items being identified or not doesn't affect your ability to
>name them.

Also, it is possible to "unname" a scroll (or potion).  I use this in 
the following scenario.

 - I pick up a scroll/potion from a shop, and name it what costs (eg:$400)
 - Later, I determine its blessed cursed status and decide to read/quaff it
   without identifying it (this is somewhat risky, but if you know it's 
   price, and you've already identified a few other "bad" scrolls/potions
   you may decide it's worth the risk.  Particularly for potions).
 - Before quaffing/reading, I unname it (Rename it and enter a space when
   asked for the name).  This ensures that when I read/quaff, I get 
   prompted again to name it.  Why?  Well, some scrolls/potions don't
   identify themselves when you use them, so you may want to record what
   the effect was. (If I forget to do this, I write a yellow sticky note 
   about what I think $400 is).
 - This may result in me having scrolls named "$400, no effect, Taming?"
   or something equally strange.

I'd given some thought to writing a change for the nethack interface which
would automagically record all the things I normally have to write on 
little yellow stickies.  Things I've considered:

1.Allowing you to edit what you've named items as even if you don't have the
  item currently in your possesion (mainly to deal with forgetting to unname
  a potion before you quaff it as above).

2.Recording a list of the levels which you've visited.  Kind of like the
  list of levels you get in wizard mode for ALL levels, but would also 
  give some highlights of each level (showing things which you have
  actually found, not all the things on the level).  Something like 
  this:

  Dungeons of Doom 1-??
    2: stairs to gnomish mines, 3{, 2#
    3: armor shop (deserted), 3{, altar (neutral)
    4: shirne (lawful, descrated), 2{, \, lev.tel.

2a. An alternative to (2) would be to create some kind of "where is" 
    command. ie: you ask where is, and tell the game what you're looking 
    for (eg: a level teleport trap), and the game tells you which levels 
    you've discovered them on.

3. A list of monsters you've genocided.  (which you can access from 
   within the genocide monster query).

I actually coded (3) for nh3.0j, but haven't got around to recoding it
for 3.1.3.  The others I haven't got around to at all.
